  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nRemainder of Labor Day Weekend...The prevailing high pressure and \nupper-level ridging will keep dry conditions in place for this \nforecast period. The only minor impact would be the potential for \npatchy fog to develop late tonight into Monday morning, but the \npotential for dense fog is low. Temperatures will remain above \nnormal with overnight lows in the mid 50s to low 60s, and highs on \nLabor Day ranging from the upper 70s to low 80s.\n\nLate Monday through Late Tuesday Precip/Storm Potential...The ridge \nand high pressure will gradually depart late Monday as return flow \nsets up ahead of an approaching system/cold front. Ahead of the \nfront, a southwesterly LLJ (35-45 kts) and increasing WAA will aid \nin rain showers and a few elevated storms starting late Monday night \nin north-central WI and spreading/moving east/southeast across the \nremainder of the forecast area through late Tuesday night. It \nappears the majority of the precipitation will be pre-frontal. Given \nthe greater instability, stronger sheer, steeper mid-level lapse \nrates will be associated with the cold front, this leads to a \nconditional risk of strong/severe storms sometime Tuesday \nevening/night, but will depend on frontal timing and how much cloud \ncover will be around from the morning precipitation. There will also \nbe an increased risk of heavy rainfall/downpours given PWATs will be \naround 1.75-2.00 inches throughout the period. In addition to the \nprecip/storm potential, breezy south winds are expected on Tuesday \ngusting to 20-25 mph, followed by westerly winds on Wednesday \ngusting to 30-35 mph.\n\nRest of the forecast...Thursday and Friday are expected to be dry \nwith the next chance of showers/storms on Friday evening into \nSaturday. Near normal temperatures are anticipated on Thursday, but \nwill rise above normal again Friday into Saturday.",
